**Version Used**:

.NET SDK 11.0.100-preview.6.26359.118. The relevant code paths look unchanged on `main`, but I only verified against this SDK.

**Steps to Reproduce**:

`.editorconfig`:

```ini
root = true

[*.cs]
dotnet_diagnostic.IDE0055.severity = warning
csharp_new_line_before_open_brace = all
```

`all` is the default, so leaving the option unset reproduces this identically.

`Program.cs`:

```csharp
public record Foo(string A, string B)
{
    public string? Path { get; init; }
}

public record Bar(Foo Inner)
{
    public string? Name { get; init; }
}

public class C
{
    public void M()
    {
        // OK - nothing wraps
        var a = Make("a", "b") with { Path = "x" };

        // IDE0055 - the wrap is in the argument list
        var b = Make(
            "a",
            "b") with { Path = "x" };

        // IDE0055 - the wrap is in the receiver chain
        var c = this
            .Make2("a", "b") with { Path = "x" };

        // IDE0055 on the OUTER brace - the wrap is inside a nested expression
        var d = new Bar(new Foo(
            "a",
            "b")) { Name = "y" };

        // OK - the statement wraps, but the creation expression does not
        var e =
            new Foo("a", "b") { Path = "x" };

        _ = (a, b, c, d, e);
    }

    private static Foo Make(string x, string y) => new(x, y);

    private Foo Make2(string x, string y) => new(x, y);
}
```

Then `dotnet build -p:EnforceCodeStyleInBuild=true`.

**Diagnostic Id**:

`IDE0055: Fix formatting`

**Expected Behavior**:

`a` through `e` all have an initializer that is entirely on one line, so all five should be treated the same way. Since `a` and `e` are accepted, `b`, `c`, and `d` should be too.

**Actual Behavior**:

`b`, `c`, and `d` are reported. The initializer is byte-identical across them; what differs is only where a line break happens to fall somewhere else in the enclosing expression — in the argument list, in the receiver chain, or (for `d`) inside a *nested* expression, leaving the reported `{ Name = "y" }` untouched.

`dotnet format whitespace` resolves them by moving the brace out but leaving the body inline, which is a shape the formatter would not produce for an initializer written across lines in the first place:

```csharp
        var b = Make(
            "a",
            "b") with
        { Path = "x" };

        var c = this
            .Make2("a", "b") with
        { Path = "x" };
```

**Analysis**:

The newline operation itself is unconditional once the flag is set, in [`NewLineUserSettingFormattingRule`](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/1f666946bae311c01eec934125d848e423bd3e12/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/CSharp/Formatting/Rules/NewLineUserSettingFormattingRule.cs#L346-L352). What keeps `a` intact is the separate single-line suppression in [`SuppressFormattingRule`](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/blob/1f666946bae311c01eec934125d848e423bd3e12/src/Workspaces/SharedUtilitiesAndExtensions/Compiler/CSharp/Formatting/Rules/SuppressFormattingRule.cs#L391-L395):

```csharp
// make creation node itself to not break into multiple line, if it is on same line
AddSuppressWrappingIfOnSingleLineOperation(list, parent.GetFirstToken(…), parent.GetLastToken(…));
```

`parent` is `initializer.Parent`, so the span tested is the whole creation expression rather than the initializer whose brace the option governs. That span strictly contains the initializer's own span, and every case in the difference — parent multi-line, initializer single-line — is one where the rule fires on exactly the one-liner the suppression exists to protect.

Keying the suppression on `initializer.OpenBraceToken … initializer.CloseBraceToken` would leave `b`, `c`, and `d` alone while still reporting an initializer that genuinely spans lines with its brace trailing:

```csharp
var f = new Foo("a", "b") {   // still IDE0055
    Path = "x"
};
```

`with` initializers behave identically to object initializers in every case above, though I could not find the corresponding suppression for them — `WithExpressionSyntax` does not appear anywhere under `Formatting/`, so they reach the same outcome by a path I did not track down.

**Related**:

- #21943 — `csharp_new_line_before_open_brace` has no per-context granularity, so there is no way to opt out of this for initializers without also giving up brace-on-its-own-line for the initializers that should have it.
- #72710 — same option and same construct, reported from the other direction (the single-line case not warning).

